1️⃣ Functional Components (Modern Way) — ✅ Recommended
📦 Structure:
A functional component is just a plain JavaScript function that returns JSX.


function Welcome(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}</h1>;
}
Or with arrow function:


const Welcome = ({ name }) => <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;



🧠 Features:

Simpler & cleaner syntax

Stateless originally, but now supports state and lifecycle via Hooks like:

useState for state

useEffect for lifecycle

🔧 With useState:

import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count = {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+</button>
    </div>
  );
}



2️⃣ Class Components (Old Way) — ❌ Not Preferred for New Code

📦 Structure:
A class component is a JavaScript class that extends React.Component and uses a render() method.


import React, { Component } from 'react';

class Welcome extends Component {
  render() {
    return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}</h1>;
  }
}


🧠 Features:
Supports state via this.state

Supports lifecycle methods like:

componentDidMount()

componentDidUpdate()

componentWillUnmount()

🔧 With state:



class Counter extends React.Component {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.state = { count: 0 };
  }

  increment = () => {
    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <p>Count = {this.state.count}</p>
        <button onClick={this.increment}>+</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}





🔗 What are Hooks in React?
Hooks are special functions in React that let you use state, side effects, and other React features in functional components (without using class components).

Introduced in React 16.8, Hooks let you "hook into" React features like state, lifecycle methods, context, refs, and more — directly in functional components.

🧠 Why Use Hooks?
✅ Allows using state in functional components

✅ Makes code simpler and reusable

✅ Avoids the complexity of class components

✅ Enables logic reuse via custom hooks




🔧 List of Most Commonly Used React Hooks (with detailed explanations)


1️⃣ useState() – To manage state in a functional component

import { useState } from 'react';

const Counter = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count = {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+</button>
    </div>
  );
};
✅ Purpose:
Create state variables in a function.

Returns an array with current value & setter function.



2️⃣ useEffect() – For side effects (like API calls, timers, event listeners)

import { useEffect } from 'react';

useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Component Mounted");

  return () => {
    console.log("Cleanup code here");
  };
}, []);
✅ Purpose:
Run code after render (e.g. fetch data, subscriptions)

Runs:

Once (on mount) if [] is passed

Every time dependencies change [dep1, dep2]

On cleanup (like componentWillUnmount)



3️⃣ useContext() – Access context value without manually passing props

const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
✅ Purpose:
Allows consuming data from React Context API.

Avoids prop drilling for deeply nested components.



4️⃣ useRef() – For DOM access or persisting a value without re-render

const inputRef = useRef();

const focusInput = () => {
  inputRef.current.focus();
};
✅ Purpose:
Reference DOM elements (like document.getElementById)

Store mutable values that don’t trigger re-render



5️⃣ useMemo() – Memoize a computed value to improve performance

const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {
  return computeSomething(num);
}, [num]);
✅ Purpose:
Cache the result of a computation to avoid recalculating it unless dependencies change



6️⃣ useCallback() – Memoize a function to prevent re-creation

const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  console.log("Clicked");
}, []);
✅ Purpose:
Cache a function and prevent re-renders of child components that receive this function as a prop



7️⃣ useReducer() – For managing complex state logic (like Redux)

const reducer = (state, action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "increment":
      return { count: state.count + 1 };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { count: 0 });
✅ Purpose:
Alternative to useState for complex state logic

Best for toggles, counters, forms, etc.

